The drake emitter refuses once, ahead of rendering; the section transforms render their own elements (GitHub #11)

OWNING ISSUE: GitHub #11 (open; stays open). Nothing on the tracker was touched.

WHY. After ddaec87 one rendering dependency was left: DracoAspectOf, RuleAspectOf, ActorAspectOf and DefinitionOf each
took `elementText ((TypeElement, Int) -> [Text])`, and the emitter supplied `elementLines`, by then two steps:
`refuseElement(element)`, then `ElementOf(element, level).lines`. The transforms could not call ElementOf themselves only
because the refusal was interleaved with the rendering, one element at a time. Dev accepted "Accepted, build it".

WHAT.
- Drake.scala: `refuseDefinition(td)` walks every element the surface renders — the type section's elements, a present
  factory's parameters and body, the globals, a present rule's variables, conditions and action, a present actor's three
  blocks — through the existing `refuseElement`. `dracoAspectText`, `ruleAspectText`, `actorAspectText` and
  `definitionText` call it before delegating; `emit` reaches it through `definitionText`, after its three codec guards.
  `elementLines` is removed.
- gendrake.DracoAspectOf, RuleAspectOf, ActorAspectOf: the `elementText` parameter removed; the binding is
  `loc elementText ((TypeElement, Int) -> [Text]) \element level -> ElementOf(element, level).lines`.
- gendrake.DefinitionOf: `DefinitionOf(definition)`, calling the three with the definition alone; its `uses` record loses
  `draco TypeElement`.
- Four surfaces authored in drake, their JSON `DrakeCLI parse`, their Scala `GeneratorCLI generate`; all stable on
  regeneration with the recompiled engine.
- Tests: DracoAspectTest asserts an absent factory's body is not rendered, through the transform and through the
  emitter; SlotOfTest builds DracoAspectOf from the definition alone. No test added, no corpus type added.

WHAT IS THE SAME. The same malformed definitions are refused with the same messages; an element the surface does not
render — an absent factory's, an absent role's — is not judged. ONE DIFFERENCE: a malformed element is refused before
any section renders, where it was refused on reaching its own section. Nothing observes it; a refusal raises either way.

WHAT STAYS hand-written on the emit side: the three codec guards and the refusals (`refuseDefinition`, `refuseElement`,
`refuseUnrenderable`, `bareKeyword`), and `typeSurface`, a one-line delegate DrakeParseTest reads. NOT BUILT, not
accepted: a failure form for the guards and refusals.

